Dengue fever
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Biphasic high fever, Severe retro-orbital headache, 'Breakbone' severe myalgia/arthralgia, Maculopapular rash with islands of sparing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-006
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of high fever, excruciating generalized pain, and recurrent vomiting requiring urgent assessment for hypovolaemia and bleeding diathesis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an incapacitating febrile illness with severe retro-orbital headache and an inability to tolerate oral fluids. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate hydration status, rule out life-threatening intracranial pathology, and screen for dengue warning signs (plasma leakage, bleeding) prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head feels like it's going to split open behind my eyes, and every bone in my body aches."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 72 hours ago with a sudden onset of high-grade, biphasic fever. Patient describes excruciating, severe generalized myalgia and deep bone/joint pain, accompanied by a severe retro-orbital headache that worsens distinctly with eye movement. A transient, diffuse macular blanching rash appeared early in the illness and is now evolving into a maculopapular rash with islands of white sparing. Patient reports severe fatigue, nausea, recurrent vomiting, and profound anorexia. Returned from endemic travel 7 days ago.
Functional Status: Bedbound, completely reliant on spouse for mobility and basic care due to severe pain and prostration.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of undetected clinical deterioration due to extreme lethargy; spouse is present but struggling to manage the patient's symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 110 | Temp: 39.2 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Acutely ill, diaphoretic, wincing with minimal movement, shielding eyes from ambient light.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals intact cranial nerves without meningism. Abdomen is soft, non-tender, with no clinical hepatomegaly or ascites. Skin examination reveals a generalized maculopapular rash with islands of sparing on the trunk and limbs. Positive tourniquet test with 15 petechiae per square inch. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Biphasic high fever, Severe retro-orbital headache, 'Breakbone' severe myalgia/arthralgia, and Maculopapular rash with islands of sparing.
Environmental Observations: Room is darkened; clear access to the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient inappropriately took ibuprofen for pain; advised to cease immediately.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Dengue fever - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Malaria - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of cyclical tertian/quartan fever spikes and prominent rash, though requires formal thick/thin film exclusion.

Meningococcal Septicaemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of non-blanching purpuric rash, nuchal rigidity, and altered sensorium.

Chikungunya - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prominence of retro-orbital pain and positive tourniquet test, though the geographical exposure and severe arthralgia are shared features.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe febrile illness and profound prostration following travel to an endemic region. The presence of a positive tourniquet test, borderline hypotension, and tachycardia raises a high index of suspicion for impending plasma leakage or bleeding complications characteristic of severe dengue. Delaying clinical evaluation and cessation of contraindicated NSAIDs until the next in-hours period carried a substantial risk of progression to hypovolemic shock or massive hemorrhage. This acute deterioration trajectory strictly fulfills the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate cessation of all NSAIDs. Prescribe paracetamol for analgesia and antipyresis. Urgent referral for FBC to assess for marked leukopenia, profound thrombocytopenia, and haematocrit elevation, alongside viral NS1 antigen/serology testing.
Positive Behavioral Change: By utilizing a 1-liter marked water bottle and drinking exactly 250mL every hour, the patient will systematically replace fluid deficits; achieving this hourly goal will result in decreased dizziness and an observable improvement in urine color, reinforcing strict adherence to the hydration regimen.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Emesis bags.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or pathology review within 12-24 hours for serial FBC monitoring.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient develops severe abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, mucosal bleeding (bleeding gums, nosebleeds, haematemesis), rapid breathing, or extreme lethargy/restlessness as the fever breaks.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending definitive serology and FBC; conditions are dynamic and can rapidly deteriorate as the fever resolves.
Clinical Course: The critical phase of dengue occurs when the fever breaks (days 3-7). Plasma leakage and bleeding risks are highest during this 48-hour window. Severe fatigue may persist for weeks following acute recovery.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The most dangerous time is actually when your fever goes away. Go to the emergency department immediately if you start bleeding from your gums, vomit blood, or develop severe stomach pain.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: NSAIDs like ibuprofen thin the blood and dramatically increase your risk of severe internal bleeding with this specific virus. Hydration is the only way to prevent shock as your blood vessels may become 'leaky'.
Actionable Steps: Throw away the ibuprofen. Sip fluids continuously. Inspect your skin twice daily for new, dark purple spots that do not fade when pressed.

